// 26 · HRV in 5 Minutes: what one reading can and can't tell you.
// Composition: hook = one plain statement across the slide; takeaway = line top-left,
// question flush right in the lower half; alignment = left. Insight is visual-dominant
// (dots and rolling line bleed in from the left margin).

module.exports = {
  num: 26,
  slug: "hrv-in-5-minutes",
  title: "HRV in 5 Minutes",
  category: "Sport Science & Technology",
  palette: "mint",
  hero: { layout: "dots-rolling-average", concept: "Four weeks of scattered morning readings with a calm seven-day average through them: one low morning barely moves the line; a run of low mornings does" },
  compose: { hook: "statement", takeaway: "question-right", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "One morning's HRV is mostly noise. A rolling average, read against the athlete's own normal, is the signal worth a conversation.",
  question: "When an athlete's HRV trend drops, who hears about it, and what changes?",
  caveats: `Most of the strong HRV evidence comes from endurance sports; Buchheit (2014) scopes his recommendations to "aerobic-oriented sports", so team-sport use is less settled. Plews et al. (2013) is 10 runners and correlates change scores with performance change: present it as "the weekly average tracked fitness changes, single days didn't", not as a precision figure. The rise in HRV after hard training in the heat is Buchheit et al. 2013 as reviewed in Buchheit 2014. Flatt et al. (2018) had only 5 linemen. HRV is not a diagnostic test for illness, overtraining or readiness; it is one input next to how the athlete says they feel. The chart on slide 3 is a drawn example, not athlete data.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "plews2013", cite: "Plews DJ, Laursen PB, Kilding AE, Buchheit M. Evaluating training adaptation with heart-rate measures: a methodological comparison. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2013;8(6):688-691. doi:10.1123/ijspp.8.6.688", grade: "small longitudinal study (10 runners)", found: "external", supports: "Change in single-day lnRMSSD was not related to change in running fitness (r = -0.06 with maximal aerobic speed); the weekly average was (r = 0.72). Averaging over a week is the better method." },
    { id: "buchheit2014", cite: "Buchheit M. Monitoring training status with HR measures: do all roads lead to Rome? Front Physiol. 2014;5:73. doi:10.3389/fphys.2014.00073", grade: "narrative review", found: "external", supports: "Interpret changes against measurement error and training context; after heavy training in the heat HRV can rise, not fall (Buchheit et al. 2013, as reviewed); correlations with performance appear only when 3-4+ days are averaged; HR measures can't inform on every aspect of fatigue and are best combined with logs and questionnaires." },
    { id: "thorpe2017", cite: "Thorpe RT, Atkinson G, Drust B, Gregson W. Monitoring fatigue status in elite team-sport athletes: implications for practice. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2017;12(Suppl 2):S2-27-S2-34. doi:10.1123/ijspp.2016-0434", grade: "narrative review", found: "Zotero", supports: "HR-derived indices are better interpreted as weekly or 7-day rolling averages than single data points because of high day-to-day variation (p. S2-28)." },
    { id: "flatt2018", cite: "Flatt AA, Esco MR, Allen JR, Robinson JB, Earley RL, Fedewa MV, Bragg A, Keith CM, Wingo JE. Heart rate variability and training load among National Collegiate Athletic Association Division 1 college football players throughout spring camp. J Strength Cond Res. 2018;32(11):3127-3134. doi:10.1519/JSC.0000000000002241", grade: "single-team observational study (25 players; 5 linemen)", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Sanders 2020)", supports: "Linemen's HRV had not returned to baseline after about 20 hours between consecutive-day sessions; heavier players showed larger drops." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Most of us have seen an HRV number by now, on an athlete's watch, a ring, or one of our own reports. HRV is heart rate variability: the small changes in time between heartbeats, measured at rest, usually first thing in the morning. It moves with recovery, stress and training.

Here is the one thing I want you to remember. A single morning's reading, on its own, tells you very little. Let me show you why, and what does tell you something.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("One morning’s HRV reading tells you very little.", { y: 2.05, w: 11.6, h: 2.3, size: 62 });
        s.source("Plews et al. 2013 · Buchheit 2014", { y: 5.95, w: 8, refs: ["plews2013", "buchheit2014"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The problem is noise. HRV bounces from one morning to the next for all kinds of reasons. In one study of runners, single-day readings had no relationship with who actually got fitter over nine weeks. The weekly average did. It was a small study, ten runners, but it matches what the reviews say.

The direction can fool you too. After heavy training in the heat, HRV has been seen to go up, not down, even when athletes felt worse.

And to be straight with you: most of the strong evidence comes from endurance sports. In team sports like ours it is useful, but less settled.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Daily readings are noisy, and the direction can mislead.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Single days didn’t track fitness changes. Weekly averages did.", "Plews et al. 2013"],
          ["After hard training in the heat, HRV can rise, not fall.", "Buchheit 2014"],
          ["Most strong evidence comes from endurance sports.", "Buchheit 2014"],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([t, src], i) => {
          const y = 2.55 + i * 1.2;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 9.3, 0.9, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
          s.source(src, { x: 10.2, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.38, h: 0.45, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top" });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.15, 12.58, 6.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("plews2013", "buchheit2014");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So read the trend, not the morning. Each dot here is one morning. The line is a seven-day rolling average, which is what the reviews recommend for exactly this reason: day-to-day swings are large, and averaging cancels much of that out.

Look at the early low dot. On its own, that morning might set off alarms. But the line barely moves. Later in the month there is a run of low mornings. That pulls the line down out of this athlete's usual range, and that is worth a conversation.

This is a drawn example, not one of our athletes.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Read the trend, not the morning.", { y: 1.2, w: 8, h: 0.75, size: 40 });
        const vals = [0.62, 0.50, 0.58, 0.47, 0.60, 0.30, 0.63, 0.53, 0.61, 0.49, 0.58, 0.52, 0.64, 0.51, 0.52, 0.44, 0.48, 0.37, 0.42, 0.33, 0.40, 0.36, 0.45, 0.41, 0.51, 0.46, 0.56, 0.52];
        const ox = 0.75, w = 10.0, X = i => ox + (i / (vals.length - 1)) * w;
        const Y = v => 5.75 - (v - 0.25) * 7.2;
        // Usual range for this athlete (illustrative).
        s.rect(ox, Y(0.61), w, Y(0.47) - Y(0.61), { fill: "soft" });
        s.text(ox + w + 0.15, Y(0.61) - 0.02, 1.7, 0.75, "Usual\nrange", { font: "cond", size: 16, color: "muted", lineH: 1.05 });
        vals.forEach((v, i) => s.circle(X(i), Y(v), 0.065, { fill: "muted" }));
        const avg = [];
        for (let i = 6; i < vals.length; i++) avg.push([X(i), Y(vals.slice(i - 6, i + 1).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / 7)]);
        s.path(avg, { smooth: true, color: "accent", w: 5 });
        s.text(X(5) + 0.18, Y(0.30) - 0.12, 3.2, 0.4, "One low morning", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "ink" });
        const end = avg[avg.length - 1];
        s.text(ox + w + 0.15, end[1] + 0.25, 1.75, 0.8, "7-day\naverage", { font: "condBold", size: 18, color: "accent", lineH: 1.05 });
        s.text(X(17) - 1.6, Y(0.30) + 0.05, 3.6, 0.4, "A run of low mornings", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "ink", align: "center" });
        s.text(ox, 5.92, 4, 0.36, "28 mornings →", { font: "light", size: 15, color: "muted" });
        s.text(ox + w - 4, 5.92, 4, 0.36, "Illustrative shape, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Rolling averages: Thorpe et al. 2017; Plews et al. 2013", { y: 6.25, h: 0.3, refs: ["thorpe2017", "plews2013"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `Three habits make the trend worth reading.

Measure the same way every time: same time of morning, same position, before coffee and before the phone. Small changes in routine show up as changes in the number.

Compare each athlete with their own normal, not with teammates. Bigger athletes can respond differently: in one Division I spring camp, linemen had not bounced back to baseline after about twenty hours between sessions. Only five linemen, so treat it as a hint, not a rule.

And when the trend drops, talk to the athlete before touching the plan. How they slept and how they feel belongs next to the number.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Three habits make the trend worth reading.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const lines = [
          "Same time, same position, every morning.",
          "Compare each athlete with their own normal, not the team.",
          "When the trend drops, ask how they feel before changing the plan.",
        ];
        lines.forEach((t, i) => s.text(0.75, 2.75 + i * 1.12, 11.4, 0.6, t, { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.1 }));
        s.source("Measurement routine and context: Buchheit 2014 · Linemen in spring camp: Flatt et al. 2018", { y: 6.0, w: 11, refs: ["buchheit2014", "flatt2018"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `So the takeaway is short: trends, not mornings. One low reading is a reason to look again tomorrow. A run of them, against that athlete's own normal, is a reason to talk.

Question for the room: when an athlete's HRV trend drops, who hears about it, and what changes? If the honest answer is "nobody" or "it depends who saw it," that is the gap worth closing.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Trends, not mornings.", { y: 1.35, w: 8, h: 1.2, size: 80 });
        s.text(5.6, 3.9, 6.98, 1.9, "When an athlete’s HRV trend drops, who hears about it, and what changes?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

