// 27 · GPS Load Data: what "high-speed running" actually measures.
// Composition: hook = a quote set right with its source beneath; takeaway = line centered,
// question beneath; alignment = center. Insight is headline-dominant (56pt headline, hero
// small in the bottom third, centered).

module.exports = {
  num: 27,
  slug: "gps-load-data",
  title: "GPS Load Data",
  category: "Sport Science & Technology",
  palette: "gold",
  hero: { layout: "speed-threshold-scale", concept: "One speed axis with a common high-speed line at 12.3 mph, and two top speeds against it: the same line sits at 88% of an offensive lineman's top speed and 61% of a wide receiver's" },
  compose: { hook: "quote-right", takeaway: "centered", align: "center" },
  oneIdea: "High-speed running is a line someone drew. Before using the number, ask where the line is and whose speed it fits.",
  question: "Which of our athletes never cross the high-speed line, and what are we missing about them?",
  caveats: `The 14.4-21.1 km/h range and the 19.8 km/h "most common" line come from studies of men's professional soccer (Gualtieri et al. 2023). Studies of women use lower lines (12.2-15.6 km/h). Our GPS provider and settings may differ; check them before quoting a number. The top speeds on slide 3 are NFL "positional maximum velocities" (Sanchez et al. 2023): the 75th percentile of game top speeds for each position, not a single player and not college players. The 88% and 61% figures are our arithmetic, combining the soccer line with the NFL speeds; mph values are converted from km/h. Malone et al. (2017) is one squad of 37 Gaelic footballers; it shows an association, and both too little and too much near-max exposure carried more risk. Absolute lines still have a use: comparing sessions and matches across a team.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "gualtieri2023", cite: "Gualtieri A, Rampinini E, Dello Iacono A, Beato M. High-speed running and sprinting in professional adult soccer: current thresholds definition, match demands and training strategies. A systematic review. Front Sports Act Living. 2023;5:1116293. doi:10.3389/fspor.2023.1116293", grade: "systematic review (30 studies)", found: "external (cited in Zotero items, e.g. Dawson 2024)", supports: "No consensus on absolute thresholds; high-speed running entry ranged 14.4-21.1 km/h in men (most common 19.8 km/h) and 12.2-15.6 km/h in women; relative thresholds suit sessions aimed at near-maximal speed." },
    { id: "sanchez2023", cite: "Sanchez E, Weiss L, Williams T, Ward P, Peterson B, Wellman A, Crandall J. Positional movement demands during NFL football games: a 3-year review. Appl Sci. 2023;13(16):9278. doi:10.3390/app13169278", grade: "large observational tracking study (NFL, 2018-2020)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Positional maximum velocity (75th percentile of game top speeds): offensive line 22.5 km/h, wide receivers 32.5 km/h (Table 2). Quote: 'An absolute threshold may indicate that certain positions never achieve sprinting velocities' (p. 3)." },
    { id: "akenhead2016", cite: "Akenhead R, Nassis GP. Training load and player monitoring in high-level football: current practice and perceptions. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2016;11(5):587-593. doi:10.1123/ijspp.2015-0331", grade: "practitioner survey (41 clubs)", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Dawson 2024)", supports: "Distance above 5.5 m/s (19.8 km/h) was among the five most-used load variables in high-level football clubs." },
    { id: "malone2017", cite: "Malone S, Roe M, Doran DA, Gabbett TJ, Collins K. High chronic training loads and exposure to bouts of maximal velocity running reduce injury risk in elite Gaelic football. J Sci Med Sport. 2017;20(3):250-254. doi:10.1016/j.jsams.2016.08.005", grade: "single-squad prospective cohort (37 players)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Players who reached over 95% of maximal velocity in training had lower injury risk than those reaching 85%; under- and over-exposure both raised risk (U-shaped)." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Every GPS report we look at has a line in it for high-speed running. It is one of the most used numbers in field sports.

Here is how a group of NFL performance staff put the problem when they wrote up three seasons of league tracking data. Read it slowly: "An absolute threshold may indicate that certain positions never achieve sprinting velocities."

In other words, the same line that makes a receiver look like he barely ran can make a lineman look like he never ran at all. Today is about what that line really means.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(3.95, 1.95, 8.63, 2.6, "“An absolute threshold may indicate that certain positions never achieve sprinting velocities.”", { font: "light", size: 40, lineH: 1.15, align: "right", color: "ink" });
        s.source("Sanchez et al., NFL tracking data, Applied Sciences, 2023", { x: 4.58, y: 4.85, w: 8, h: 0.4, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top", refs: ["sanchez2023"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `First problem: there is no standard line. A 2023 systematic review of thirty studies in men's professional soccer found the high-speed line set anywhere from about 9 to 13 miles per hour. The most common choice was 12.3, which is 5.5 meters per second, and that same number shows up among the most used load variables in professional clubs. Studies of women set it lower again.

So two programs can both report "high-speed running" and be counting different things. Before comparing our numbers with anyone else's, or even across seasons, ask where the line was drawn.`,
      build(s) {
