// 28 · Sleep Tracking: useful tool or anxious obsession?
// Composition: hook = one big question, nothing else but a source; takeaway = two columns
// split by a vertical rule (line | question); alignment = left. Insight is visual-dominant
// (2x2 hugging the right margin, headline top-left).

module.exports = {
  num: 28,
  slug: "sleep-tracking",
  title: "Sleep Tracking",
  category: "Sport Science & Technology",
  palette: "slate",
  hero: { layout: "two-by-two-quadrant", concept: "What the athlete looks at (weekly patterns vs nightly scores) against how it leaves them (curious vs anxious): patterns with curiosity help; nightly scores with worry hurt" },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "split-columns", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Sleep trackers are decent at patterns and weak at grading one night. Use them for trends, and watch for the athlete the score is making anxious.",
  question: "Who do our athletes talk to when a sleep score worries them?",
  caveats: `Chinoy et al. (2021) tested 34 healthy young adults in a lab with older device models; newer devices may do better, and athletes may differ. The point that holds is the direction of error: devices tend to call quiet wake time sleep. Baron et al. (2017) is three case reports: it names a pattern ("orthosomnia"), not how common it is. Gavriloff et al. (2018) studied adults with insomnia disorder and compared two sham conditions (told their sleep was worse vs better); there was no "true feedback" group, and athletes without insomnia may react less. The 2x2 on slide 3 is a way to sort conversations, not a finding. Ongoing sleep problems are a clinical matter for Athletic Medicine and Baylor's licensed mental health staff, not something to manage from an app.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "chinoy2021", cite: "Chinoy ED, Cuellar JA, Huwa KE, Jameson JT, Watson CH, Bessman SC, Hirsch DA, Cooper AD, Drummond SPA, Markwald RR. Performance of seven consumer sleep-tracking devices compared with polysomnography. Sleep. 2021;44(5):zsaa291. doi:10.1093/sleep/zsaa291", grade: "laboratory validation study (34 healthy adults, 3 nights)", found: "external", supports: "Epoch-by-epoch sensitivity (sleep detected as sleep) was 0.93 or higher for all devices; specificity (wake detected as wake) was 0.18-0.54; sleep-stage estimates were inconsistent; devices did worse on disrupted nights." },
    { id: "baron2017", cite: "Baron KG, Abbott S, Jao N, Manalo N, Mullen R. Orthosomnia: are some patients taking the quantified self too far? J Clin Sleep Med. 2017;13(2):351-354. doi:10.5664/jcsm.6472", grade: "case series (3 patients)", found: "external", supports: "Coins 'orthosomnia', a preoccupation with perfecting tracker sleep data; all three patients spent extra time in bed chasing the device's number, which may have worsened their insomnia, and devices that overestimate sleep may reinforce that habit." },
    { id: "gavriloff2018", cite: "Gavriloff D, Sheaves B, Juss A, Espie CA, Miller CB, Kyle SD. Sham sleep feedback delivered via actigraphy biases daytime symptom reports in people with insomnia: implications for insomnia disorder and wearable devices. J Sleep Res. 2018;27(6):e12726. doi:10.1111/jsr.12726", grade: "randomized experiment (63 adults with insomnia)", found: "external", supports: "People given negative sham sleep feedback reported lower alertness and more sleepiness and fatigue by evening than those given positive sham feedback; no difference on vigilance testing." },
    { id: "nobari2023", cite: "Nobari H, Banihashemi M, Saedmocheshi S, Prieto-González P, Oliveira R. Overview of the impact of sleep monitoring on optimal performance, immune system function and injury risk reduction in athletes: a narrative review. Sci Prog. 2023;106(4):00368504231206265. doi:10.1177/00368504231206265", grade: "narrative review", found: "Zotero", supports: "Activity monitors use accelerometers to classify movement as sleep or wake; monitoring is useful for describing athletes' sleep over time." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 40,
      notes: `A lot of our athletes wake up and check a sleep score before they check anything else. Some of our staff do too. So here is a fair question to start with.

Can a sleep score make your sleep worse? Sleep doctors have a name for one version of this. They call it orthosomnia: a preoccupation with getting perfect numbers from a sleep tracker. Let's look at what the trackers actually measure, and when the data helps or hurts.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Can a sleep score make your sleep worse?", { y: 1.75, w: 10.2, h: 3.0, size: 84 });
        s.source("“Orthosomnia”: Baron et al., J Clin Sleep Med, 2017", { y: 5.95, w: 8, refs: ["baron2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Start with what the devices can do. In a careful lab study, researchers put seven consumer trackers up against a full sleep lab recording. Every device was good at recognizing sleep: 93 percent or better. Recognizing wake was another story. They caught between 18 and 54 percent of the time people were actually awake, so they tend to count lying still as sleeping. Sleep-stage estimates, the deep and REM numbers, were inconsistent. And they did worse on bad nights, which is exactly when people look hardest at the score.

That was a lab study of healthy adults with older models. Newer ones may be better. But the direction of the error is worth knowing.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.9, 0, 4.433, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("Trackers are good at sleep and poor at wake.", { y: 1.2, w: 7.4, h: 1.7, size: 48 });
        s.lead("In a lab test of seven devices, all caught 93%+ of sleep but only 18–54% of wake. Stage estimates were inconsistent.", { y: 3.35, w: 7.2, h: 1.9, size: 26, color: "ink" });
        s.text(9.35, 2.6, 3.23, 0.6, "Asleep: caught.", { font: "cond", size: 30, color: "ink" });
        s.text(9.35, 3.45, 3.23, 0.6, "Awake: often missed.", { font: "cond", size: 30, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Chinoy et al., Sleep, 2021", { y: 5.95, w: 7.2, refs: ["chinoy2021"] });
        s.cite("nobari2023");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So how should we think about it? Two questions sort most situations: what is the athlete looking at, and how does it leave them?

Weekly patterns, like bedtime drifting later or total sleep falling across a road trip, are what these devices do best. If the athlete is curious about that, the data helps. Top left.

A nightly score, read by someone already worried, is the risky corner. In one experiment, adults with insomnia who were told their sleep had been poor felt more tired and less alert that day than people told it had been good. Same nights, different story. Bottom right.

The other two corners are mostly about conversation. This chart is a way to sort it, not data.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("A pattern helps. A grade can hurt.", { y: 1.2, w: 4.4, h: 1.5, size: 40 });
        s.lead("Sorted by what the athlete looks at, and how it leaves them.", { y: 3.0, w: 4.0, h: 1.3, size: 20 });
        const gx = 5.75, gy = 1.75, cw = 3.4, ch = 2.05;
        s.text(gx, gy - 0.5, cw, 0.4, "Weekly patterns", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.text(gx + cw, gy - 0.5, cw, 0.4, "Nightly scores", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.rect(gx, gy, cw, ch, { fill: "soft" });
        s.line(gx, gy + ch, gx + 2 * cw, gy + ch, { color: "rule", w: 1.25 });
        s.line(gx + cw, gy, gx + cw, gy + 2 * ch, { color: "rule", w: 1.25 });
        s.text(gx - 1.15, gy + ch / 2 - 0.2, 1.05, 0.4, "Curious", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.text(gx - 1.15, gy + ch * 1.5 - 0.2, 1.05, 0.4, "Anxious", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        const q = (col, row, t, o) => s.text(gx + col * cw + 0.3, gy + row * ch + 0.55, cw - 0.6, 1.0, t, { font: "cond", size: 26, lineH: 1.05, align: "center", ...o });
        q(0, 0, "Helps.\nUse it.", { font: "condBold", color: "accent", size: 30 });
        q(1, 0, "Low stakes.", { color: "ink" });
        q(0, 1, "Talk first.", { color: "ink" });
        q(1, 1, "Hurts.\nPut it away.", { font: "condBold", color: "ink", size: 30 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.6, 4.4, 0.36, "A way to sort it, not data", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Chinoy et al. 2021 · Gavriloff et al. 2018 · Baron et al. 2017", { y: 6.0, w: 4.6, h: 0.5, refs: ["chinoy2021", "gavriloff2018", "baron2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `Here is the one thing to do differently on Monday. When an athlete brings you a bad sleep score, start with them, not the number. How do you feel? How did you sleep, in your words? If they feel fine, the score probably over-read a restless night, and you can say so.

Don't build habits around chasing a number. In the orthosomnia cases, people spent extra time in bed trying to raise their tracker's score, and their sleep got worse.

And if poor sleep goes on, that is not a coaching conversation or an app problem. Bring in Athletic Medicine, and Baylor's licensed mental health staff when stress or mood is part of it.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("If a sleep score worries an athlete, ask how they feel before you look at the number.", { y: 1.9, w: 11.0, h: 2.4, size: 40 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.6, 1.75, 4.6, { color: "rule", w: 2 });
        s.lead("Sleep trouble that lasts goes to Athletic Medicine.", { y: 4.85, w: 10, h: 0.6, size: 24 });
        s.cite("baron2017", "gavriloff2018");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 50,
      notes: `So: track patterns, don't grade nights. Used across weeks, a tracker can start a useful conversation about routines, travel and late nights. Used as a morning report card, it can make some athletes sleep worse.

Question for the room: who do our athletes talk to when a sleep score worries them? If the answer is "the app," we have work to do. Athletic Medicine, coaches and Athletic Performance all hear these worries first; we should agree on what we say.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Track patterns. Don’t grade nights.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.75, w: 5.6, h: 3.1, size: 64 });
        s.line(6.9, 1.8, 6.9, 5.9, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(7.45, 1.8, 5.1, 3.0, "Who do our athletes talk to when a sleep score worries them?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

