// 29 · Force Plates: what asymmetry data can (and can't) tell you about injury risk.
// Composition: hook = small figure bottom-right in an empty field, one-line headline
// top-left; takeaway = the question is the whole slide; alignment = right (right-aligned
// headlines, hero and panel mirrored to a right rail). Insight is headline-dominant.

module.exports = {
  num: 29,
  slug: "force-plates",
  title: "Force Plates",
  category: "Sport Science & Technology",
  palette: "paper",
  hero: { layout: "mirrored-bars-tolerance", concept: "Six test days of left-versus-right difference around a center line, with the athlete's usual swing shaded and a fixed 10% flag dashed: the flag fires on normal days; only one day falls outside her own range" },
  compose: { hook: "small-figure-field", takeaway: "question-only", align: "right" },
  oneIdea: "An asymmetry number means little on its own. Judge it against that athlete's usual test-to-test swing, not a fixed 10-15% rule.",
  question: "Which asymmetry numbers are we acting on without a baseline to compare them with?",
  caveats: `The 37% on the hook comes from weekly hip adduction strength on a fixed-frame dynamometer in 17 Premier League players (Callaway et al. 2026), not from jumps on force plates; say so out loud. It shows how often a fixed 10% flag fires in healthy players, not that asymmetry never matters. Helme et al. (2021) reviewed 31 studies: 8 found no link, 10 a partial link and 10 a significant link (their abstract leaves 3 unclassified), mostly moderate to low quality evidence. Bishop et al. (2021) is 28 recreational athletes. Asymmetry is most useful after injury, measured against a pre-injury baseline or a matched benchmark, and read with Athletic Medicine (Jordan & Bishop 2024). The bars on slide 3 are drawn for the deck, not an athlete's data.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "callaway2026", cite: "Callaway AJ, Dunne C, Williams J. Group normative data and arbitrary asymmetry thresholds fail to represent individual players in weekly hip strength monitoring. Sci Rep. 2026. doi:10.1038/s41598-026-63569-x", grade: "single-squad longitudinal study (17 players, 589 player-weeks)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Return-to-play decisions can rely on arbitrary 10-15% asymmetry thresholds with limited support; although all 17 players stayed injury-free, 37% of weekly sessions exceeded the 10% adduction asymmetry threshold. Recommends individual rolling averages." },
    { id: "helme2021", cite: "Helme M, Tee J, Emmonds S, Low C. Does lower-limb asymmetry increase injury risk in sport? A systematic review. Phys Ther Sport. 2021;49:204-213. doi:10.1016/j.ptsp.2021.03.001", grade: "systematic review (31 studies)", found: "external", supports: "8 studies found no association, 10 partial and 10 significant; overall moderate to lower quality evidence; a relationship can't be ruled out." },
    { id: "bishop2021", cite: "Bishop C, Lake J, Loturco I, Papadopoulos K, Turner A, Read P. Interlimb asymmetries: the need for an individual approach to data analysis. J Strength Cond Res. 2021;35(3):695-701. doi:10.1519/JSC.0000000000002729", grade: "cross-sectional study (28 recreational athletes)", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Bishop 2022)", supports: "Mean asymmetries were small, individual ones much larger, and the favored side rarely matched across tests (kappa <0.01-0.79)." },
    { id: "jordan2024", cite: "Jordan MJ, Bishop C. Testing limb symmetry and asymmetry after anterior cruciate ligament injury: 4 considerations to increase its utility. Strength Cond J. 2024;46(4):406-414. doi:10.1519/SSC.0000000000000821", grade: "narrative review with recommendations", found: "Zotero", supports: "Movement asymmetries are variable and task-dependent in uninjured people; establish each athlete's test-to-test reliability, test over multiple sessions, and use a pre-injury or matched benchmark (pp. 408-409)." },
    { id: "bishop2022", cite: "Bishop C, Read P, Bromley T, Brazier J, Jarvis P, Chavda S, Turner A. The association between interlimb asymmetry and athletic performance tasks: a season-long study in elite academy soccer players. J Strength Cond Res. 2022;36(3):787-795. doi:10.1519/JSC.0000000000003526", grade: "single-squad longitudinal study (18 players)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Links between jump asymmetry and speed did not track consistently across a season; no recommendation to reduce asymmetry for performance could be made." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `A Premier League team tested hip strength every week for a whole season. Seventeen players, thirty-five weeks. Every one of them stayed injury-free.

And yet in 37 percent of those weekly tests, the left-right difference crossed the ten percent line that a lot of programs use as a red flag. That was a strength frame, not a jump on force plates, but the lesson carries over.

If a rule flags healthy players more than a third of the time, the rule is the problem. So what can asymmetry data actually tell us?`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Healthy players, flagged anyway.", { y: 1.25, w: 8.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        s.figure("37%", { x: 8.9, y: 3.85, w: 3.68, h: 1.85, size: 140, align: "right", refs: ["callaway2026"] });
        s.lead("of weekly hip strength tests crossed the 10% asymmetry flag. All 17 players stayed injury-free.", { x: 3.6, y: 4.35, w: 5.0, h: 1.3, size: 20, align: "right" });
        s.source("Callaway et al., Scientific Reports, 2026 · strength frame, one Premier League squad", { x: 4.58, y: 6.0, w: 8, align: "right", refs: ["callaway2026"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is where the research stands. A 2021 systematic review looked at 31 studies of lower-limb asymmetry and injury. Some found a link, some found a partial link, and some found none, and most of the evidence was moderate to low quality. A link can't be ruled out. It isn't established either.

There is a second problem. Asymmetry is not one stable trait. In one study, the side that looked stronger in one jump test was often the weaker side in another. So "she's 12 percent off on her left" depends heavily on which test, which metric, and which day.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(0, 0, 4.444, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.text(0.75, 2.6, 3.3, 2.2, "Does asymmetry predict injury?", { font: "cond", size: 34, color: "ink", lineH: 1.08 });
        s.headline("The research answer: mixed.", { x: 5.0, y: 1.2, w: 7.58, h: 0.9, size: 44, align: "right" });
        s.line(5.0, 2.65, 12.58, 2.65, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(5.0, 2.85, 7.58, 1.4, "A review of 31 studies: some links, some partial, some none. Mostly moderate to low quality evidence.", { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
        s.line(5.0, 4.4, 12.58, 4.4, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(5.0, 4.6, 7.58, 0.95, "The “stronger” side often flips between tests.", { font: "light", size: 24, lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
        s.source("Helme et al. 2021 · Bishop et al. 2021", { x: 4.58, y: 6.0, w: 8, align: "right", refs: ["helme2021", "bishop2021"] });
        s.cite("bishop2022");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `So here is the shift. Each row is one test day for one athlete, showing how far her left and right legs differ, and which way.

The shaded band is her usual swing: how much she varies from test to test when nothing is wrong. The dashed lines are a fixed ten percent flag. Look at what happens. The flag fires on two ordinary days that sit inside her normal range. Then on the last day she moves well outside her own band. That is the day worth a retest and a conversation.

The number only means something next to that athlete's own normal. This chart is drawn for the deck, not real data.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Much of what looks like asymmetry is normal variation.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.2, w: 11.83, h: 1.95, size: 56, align: "right" });
        const cx = 9.05, k = 0.12, X = p => cx + p * k;
        const top = 3.6, rowH = 0.36;
        const vals = [6, -11, 13, -5, 9, 24];
        const bottom = top + vals.length * rowH;
        s.rect(X(-15), top - 0.12, X(15) - X(-15), bottom - top + 0.12, { fill: "soft" });
        [-10, 10].forEach(p => s.line(X(p), top - 0.12, X(p), bottom, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, dash: true }));
        s.line(cx, top - 0.12, cx, bottom, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
        vals.forEach((v, i) => {
          const y = top + i * rowH + 0.06;
          const out = Math.abs(v) > 15;
          s.rect(Math.min(cx, X(v)), y, Math.abs(X(v) - cx), 0.22, { fill: out ? "accent" : "ink", opacity: out ? 1 : 0.8 });
        });
        s.text(X(-15) - 0.05, top - 0.5, X(15) - X(-15) + 0.1, 0.34, "Her usual swing", { font: "cond", size: 16, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.text(3.0, top + 1 * rowH - 0.04, 3.4, 0.34, "Flagged, but normal for her", { font: "cond", size: 16, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.line(6.5, top + 1 * rowH + 0.15, 6.6, top + 1 * rowH + 0.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(3.0, top + 5 * rowH - 0.06, 3.4, 0.36, "Outside her normal", { font: "condBold", size: 18, color: "accent", align: "right" });
        s.text(X(10) + 0.05, bottom + 0.05, 1.6, 0.32, "10% flag", { font: "cond", size: 15, color: "muted" });
        s.text(X(-15) - 2.6, bottom + 0.05, 2.5, 0.32, "← favors left · favors right →", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.source("Illustrative shape, not data · Bishop et al. 2021 · Jordan & Bishop 2024 · Callaway et al. 2026", { x: 1.58, y: 6.22, w: 11, h: 0.32, align: "right", refs: ["bishop2021", "jordan2024", "callaway2026"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `In practice, this changes one sentence. The old sentence is "he's 12 percent asymmetric, red flag." It treats one number on one day as a verdict.

The better sentence compares him with himself, then asks for more information: retest, check whether the same side shows up in another test, and bring in Athletic Medicine before anything changes.

Asymmetry earns its keep after an injury, when we can compare the injured leg with a pre-injury baseline or a matched benchmark. Outside of that, it is a prompt to look closer, never a diagnosis. These two quotes are examples written for this deck.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Change the sentence we say.", { x: 2.58, y: 1.2, w: 10, h: 0.9, size: 44, align: "right" });
        s.label("Before", { x: 9.58, y: 2.55, w: 3, align: "right" });
        s.text(1.58, 2.95, 11.0, 0.8, "“He’s 12% asymmetric. Red flag.”", { font: "light", size: 28, color: "muted", lineH: 1.15, align: "right" });
        s.line(0.75, 4.0, 12.58, 4.0, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("After", { x: 9.58, y: 4.25, w: 3, align: "right" });
        s.text(0.75, 4.65, 11.83, 1.3, "“That’s outside his usual range. Let’s retest and loop in Athletic Medicine.”", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "ink", lineH: 1.12, align: "right" });
        s.text(5.58, 6.0, 7, 0.36, "Example messages written for this deck.", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", align: "right" });
        s.cite("jordan2024");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line I want you to keep: compare the athlete with themselves. A fixed ten or fifteen percent rule is easy to apply and easy to get wrong.

Question for the room: which asymmetry numbers are we acting on without a baseline to compare them with? Think about preseason testing, return-to-play decisions, and anything that reaches a coach as a single red cell on a sheet.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.75, 11.83, 3.6, "Which asymmetry numbers are we acting on without a baseline to compare them with?", { font: "medium", size: 52, color: "accent", lineH: 1.12, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

