input.content:
// 30 · Data to Decision: from dashboard to intervention, closing the analytics loop.
// Composition: hook = one word at 104pt with a short lead in a narrow measure beneath;
// takeaway = huge headline, short rule, question below; alignment = left. Insight is
// visual-dominant (narrowing steps centered under a one-line headline).

module.exports = {
  num: 30,
  slug: "data-to-decision",
  title: "Data to Decision",
  category: "Sport Science & Technology",
  palette: "sage",
  hero: { layout: "narrowing-steps", concept: "Data, insight, decision and action as four centered lines that shorten at each step, with a feedback arrow from action back to data: the loop only closes when what we did goes back to athletes and coaches" },
  compose: { hook: "single-word", takeaway: "headline-rule-question", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Data is only worth collecting if it reaches a decision someone owns, and the result gets reported back.",
  question: "Which report do we produce that nobody has acted on this season?",
  caveats: `The evidence here is mostly surveys and commentary, not trials. Akenhead & Nassis (2016) surveyed 41 of 82 invited professional soccer clubs; the 23- and 20-point gaps are differences on their own effectiveness rating scale, not "23% less effective". Neupert et al. (2024) is a small survey (around 25 of 75 invited Olympic and Paralympic practitioners answered the key items); the feedback-adherence link is a correlation in practitioners' reports, not proof that feedback causes adherence. Houtmeyers et al. (2021) and West et al. (2021) are commentaries. The narrowing steps on slide 3 are a model; the widths aren't measured. Don't present this as "our data is wasted": the point is to start reports from the decision they serve.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "akenhead2016", cite: "Akenhead R, Nassis GP. Training load and player monitoring in high-level football: current practice and perceptions. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2016;11(5):587-593. doi:10.1123/ijspp.2015-0331", grade: "practitioner survey (41 clubs)", found: "external (cited across Zotero items, e.g. Neupert 2024)", supports: "Actual effectiveness of monitoring was rated 23 and 20 points below expected for injury prevention and performance; the top barrier was limited human resources, then coach buy-in." },
    { id: "neupert2024", cite: "Neupert E, Holder T, Gupta L, Jobson SA. More than metrics: the role of socio-environmental factors in determining the success of athlete monitoring. J Sports Sci. 2024;42(4):323-332. doi:10.1080/02640414.2024.2330178", grade: "small practitioner survey (Olympic and Paralympic sport)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Athlete feedback correlated with athlete monitoring adherence (rS = 0.675); a scientific base was associated with better feedback (abstract)." },
    { id: "houtmeyers2021", cite: "Houtmeyers KC, Jaspers A, Figueiredo P. Managing the training process in elite sports: from descriptive to prescriptive data analytics. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2021;16:1719-1723. doi:10.1123/ijspp.2020-0958", grade: "invited commentary / framework", found: "Zotero", supports: "Four analytic steps from describing the past to guiding the future; current use of data in elite sport mostly informs decisions rather than driving them, and practitioners' expertise remains the main driver (p. 1719)." },
    { id: "west2021", cite: "West SW, Clubb J, Torres-Ronda L, Howells D, Leng E, Vescovi JD, Carmody S, Posthumus M, Dalen-Lorentsen T, Windt J. More than a metric: how training load is used in elite sport for athlete management. Int J Sports Med. 2021;42(4):300-306. doi:10.1055/a-1268-8791", grade: "editorial / consensus of practitioners", found: "Zotero", supports: "Stakeholders count on practitioners to turn data into meaningful decisions; load informs decisions from daily sessions to season planning; context comes from regular conversation with athletes (pp. 1-4)." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 40,
      notes: `One word. "So?"

That is the question a head coach asks, out loud or not, every time we hand them a chart. We now collect more about our athletes than ever: GPS, force plates, heart rate, sleep, wellness surveys. Every one of those numbers should be able to answer "so?" in a sentence.

Today is about the path from a number to something that actually changes for an athlete, and where that path usually breaks.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.45, 6, 1.9, "So?", { font: "condBold", size: 104, color: "accent", lineH: 1.0, role: "headline" });
        s.lead("Every report we hand a coach should answer that in one line.", { y: 3.75, w: 6.0, h: 1.3, size: 26 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The problem is not collecting. It is everything after. When professional soccer clubs were surveyed, they rated their monitoring well below what they expected it to deliver, for injuries and for performance. The biggest barriers were not the technology. They were too few people to use the data, then coach buy-in.

