      build(s) {
        s.headline("Collecting is the easy part.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Clubs rated monitoring less effective than they expected.", "Akenhead & Nassis 2016"],
          ["Biggest barriers: too few people, then coach buy-in.", "Akenhead & Nassis 2016"],
          ["Where athletes got feedback, they kept up with monitoring.", "Neupert et al. 2024"],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([t, src], i) => {
          const y = 2.55 + i * 1.2;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 9.1, 0.9, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1 });
          s.source(src, { x: 9.98, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.6, h: 0.45, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top" });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.15, 12.58, 6.15, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("akenhead2016", "neupert2024");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `Here is how I think about it. We start wide: data, everything we collect. Some of it becomes insight: what changed, and why. Less of that becomes a decision: what we will do differently. And the narrowest step is action: who does it, and by when.

Each step keeping less is not a failure. That is the point. Most numbers should never reach a coach.

The arrow on the right is the part we skip. When we report back what we did and what happened, athletes and coaches see why the data matters, and the next round of data gets better. Researchers describe most sport data today as informing decisions rather than driving them, which is the right order. People decide.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Each step keeps less, and that’s the point.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.6, h: 0.75, size: 40 });
        const cx = 6.4;
        const steps = [
          ["Data", "everything we collect", 10.0],
          ["Insight", "what changed, and why", 7.4],
          ["Decision", "what we’ll do differently", 4.9],
          ["Action", "who does it, by when", 2.6],
        ];
        steps.forEach(([k, d, w], i) => {
          const y = 2.75 + i * 1.0;
          const last = i === steps.length - 1;
          s.text(cx - 3.5, y - 0.6, 7, 0.45, [{ t: k, font: "condBold", size: 24, color: last ? "accent" : "ink" }, { t: ` · ${d}`, font: "light", size: 20, color: "muted" }], { align: "center", size: 20 });
          s.line(cx - w / 2, y, cx + w / 2, y, { color: last ? "accent" : "shape", w: last ? 8 : 5 });
        });
        const ax = 12.05;
        s.line(cx + 2.6 / 2 + 0.25, 5.75, ax, 5.75, { color: "muted", w: 1.5 });
        s.line(ax, 5.75, ax, 2.75, { color: "muted", w: 1.5, arrow: "end" });
        s.text(9.2, 3.55, 2.7, 0.8, "Report back:\nthe loop closes", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "ink", align: "right", lineH: 1.05 });
        s.source("Houtmeyers et al. 2021 · West et al. 2021 · feedback: Neupert et al. 2024 · a model, widths not measured", { y: 6.2, w: 11.3, refs: ["houtmeyers2021", "west2021", "neupert2024"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 70,
      notes: `So reverse the usual order. Three steps, and the order matters.

First, name the decision and who owns it. "Should this athlete do the full session Tuesday?" is owned by the sport coach with Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance. If no one owns a decision, the report has no reader.

Second, pick the one or two numbers that actually inform that decision. Not the whole dashboard.

Third, report back. Tell the coach and the athlete what we did and what happened. That is what keeps athletes filling in the forms, and it is how we learn which numbers are worth keeping.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Start from the decision, not the data.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const steps = [
          ["1", "Name the decision, and who owns it."],
          ["2", "Pick the one or two numbers that inform it."],
          ["3", "Report back what we did and what happened."],
        ];
        steps.forEach(([n, t], i) => {
          const y = 2.6 + i * 1.18;
          s.text(0.75, y, 0.8, 0.8, n, { font: "cond", size: 40, color: "muted", lineH: 0.9 });
          s.text(1.75, y + 0.06, 10.6, 0.7, t, { font: "light", size: 30, lineH: 1.1 });
        });
        s.cite("west2021", "houtmeyers2021");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `One line to take away: every number owes someone a decision. If a report has not changed anything in a season, either nobody owns the decision it serves, or it is serving no decision at all. Both are worth knowing.

Question for the room: which report do we produce that nobody has acted on this season? It is a safe question to answer honestly. Stopping a report frees up time for the ones that matter.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Every number owes someone a decision.", { y: 1.35, w: 10.5, h: 2.6, size: 80 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.55, 1.75, 4.55, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.8, 11.5, 1.0, "Which report do we produce that nobody has acted on this season?", { font: "medium", size: 28, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

